window.ISTLAB = {"lessons":{"1.1":{"id":"1.1","title":"The CIA Triad","back":"../unit-1/index.html","w":{"pts":50,"intro":"You will go through the 1.1 notes one part at a time. Each step tells you which part to open, then asks questions that only someone who read that part can answer. You need 100% to open the assignment, and you can check as many times as you like.","steps":[{"h":"The three questions","read":"<p>Open the notes and read the top of the page, from the title down to the three cards: <b>Confidentiality</b>, <b>Integrity</b> and <b>Availability</b>. Each card asks one short question and gives an analogy.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the 1.1 notes","href":"../unit-1/1-1-notes.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q1","type":"match","q":"Match each pillar to the analogy the notes use for it.","left":["Confidentiality","Integrity","Availability"],"right":["The power and water at your house","A safety seal on a food container","A sealed letter or a diary with a lock"]},{"id":"q2","type":"mc","q":"Which of the three short questions on the cards goes with <b>Integrity</b>?","opts":["Is it accurate?","Is it free to use?","Is it there when I need it?","Is it secret?"]},{"id":"q3","type":"multi","q":"The first paragraph says you already practice cybersecurity every day. Which three everyday things does it name?","opts":["Changing your ringtone","Charging your phone overnight","Getting annoyed when Wi-Fi drops","Locking your phone","Checking your bank balance"]}]},{"h":"Part 01: Confidentiality","read":"<p>Scroll to <b>01: C, Confidentiality</b>. Read all ten examples and the red <b>When it fails</b> paragraph under them.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the 1.1 notes","href":"../unit-1/1-1-notes.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q4","type":"mc","q":"Which example is described as \"confidentiality with a built-in expiration date\"?","opts":["Two-factor authentication","A disappearing Snapchat photo","Incognito browsing","A private Instagram account"]},{"id":"q5","type":"mc","q":"The notes call one thing \"the single fastest way confidentiality actually breaks for a teenager.\" What is it?","opts":["A site that loses its security certificate overnight","A weak Wi-Fi signal in the school building","A phone left unlocked, such as lending it to a friend","A hacker in another country guessing your password"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mc","q":"According to the notes, who does Incognito or private browsing mode hide your search history from?","opts":["Your internet provider and its servers","Every website you visit during the session","Your school and everything on its network","Anyone else who uses the same shared device"]},{"id":"q7","type":"mc","q":"What does two-factor authentication (2FA) stop, according to the notes?","opts":["Someone logging in with only your stolen password","Your phone from running out of battery power","A shared file from being edited by a teammate","Anyone from ever seeing your username online"]}]},{"h":"Part 02: Integrity","read":"<p>Scroll to <b>02: I, Integrity</b>. Read all ten examples and the <b>When it fails</b> paragraph. This is the pillar the notes say most people forget about.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the 1.1 notes","href":"../unit-1/1-1-notes.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q8","type":"mc","q":"Why does Google Docs <b>version history</b> matter on a group project, according to the notes?","opts":["It makes a long document load faster on slow Wi-Fi","It can catch someone quietly rewriting a teammate's paragraph","It keeps the document online during an outage","It keeps your document secret from every outsider"]},{"id":"q9","type":"mc","q":"A friend sends a screenshot as \"proof\" you said something. The notes say the image itself can be a problem for which pillar?","opts":["None of the pillars, since a screenshot is only a picture","Availability, since a large image is slow to open","Integrity, since screenshots can be edited before sending","Confidentiality, since the screenshot shows a private chat"]},{"id":"q10","type":"multi","q":"Which of these does the notes' Integrity list include as examples of data being changed or faked? Pick all that apply.","opts":["School Wi-Fi going down before a deadline","A deepfaked video or Photoshopped photo","A text thread forwarded without the rest of the conversation","A phone left unlocked at lunch","Autocorrect changing what you typed"]}]},{"h":"Part 03: Availability","read":"<p>Scroll to <b>03: A, Availability</b>. Read all ten examples and the <b>When it fails</b> paragraph.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the 1.1 notes","href":"../unit-1/1-1-notes.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q11","type":"mc","q":"The notes say a system can be \"perfectly private and perfectly accurate\" and still be useless. When?","opts":["When it is stored in only one language","When its password is much too long to type","When it has more users than expected","When it is down and you cannot reach it"]},{"id":"q12","type":"mc","q":"Why does the notes list ransomware locking a school's computers under <b>Availability</b>?","opts":["The files stay, but nobody can open them without paying","The school forgot the password to its own Wi-Fi network","The attackers quietly changed the grades in the portal","The attackers read every file on the system in secret"]},{"id":"q13","type":"mc","q":"During a DDoS attack, the notes say the website is \"up\" but what?","opts":["Turned into a private site that needs a login","Unreachable, because fake traffic crowds out real visitors","Showing visitors information that was changed by someone","Secretly copied onto the attacker's own server"]}]},{"h":"Part 04: Putting it together","read":"<p>Scroll to <b>04: Putting It Together</b>. Read the table and the paragraph about the three pillars pulling against each other.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the 1.1 notes","href":"../unit-1/1-1-notes.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q14","type":"mc","q":"The notes use a hospital as an example of a tradeoff. What was traded away?","opts":["Nothing, because the setup was perfect","Integrity, to get more availability","Availability, to get more confidentiality","Confidentiality, to get more integrity"]},{"id":"q15","type":"num","q":"How many examples does the notes list for each pillar?"},{"id":"q16","type":"mc","q":"What does the notes say good security is about?","opts":["Protecting confidentiality above everything else","Using as many different passwords as possible","Getting a perfect score on every single pillar","Choosing the right balance for the situation"]},{"id":"q17","type":"write","q":"In your own words, explain why pushing one pillar too hard can cost another. Use the hospital example from the notes. (2 to 3 sentences)","min":25}]}]},"a":{"pts":100,"company":"Spotify","color":"#1DB954","tagline":"You are a summer security analyst on Spotify's trust team. Your manager sends you the week's incident reports.","from":"Maya Brooks, Security Team Lead","subject":"Monday triage: six incidents to label by pillar","email":"<p>Welcome to the team. Every incident that comes in gets labeled with the pillar of the CIA Triad that failed, so the right engineers see it first.</p><p>Below are this week's reports and a few decisions I need from you. Work through them in order. This is a classroom simulation, so the details are made up.</p>","steps":[{"h":"Label the incidents","read":"<p>Match each incident report to the pillar that failed.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q1","type":"match","q":"Match each incident to the pillar that failed.","left":["A hacker reads the private playlists and listening history of 40,000 users.","Someone edits the play counts on a song, so the artist is paid the wrong amount.","A flood of fake traffic makes the app unreachable on release day."],"right":["Integrity","Availability","Confidentiality"]},{"id":"q2","type":"match","q":"Now three more.","left":["Ransomware locks the internal tools the support team needs.","An employee emails a customer list to the wrong person.","A fake \"verified\" artist account posts songs under a real artist's name."],"right":["Confidentiality","Integrity","Availability"]}]},{"h":"Decide the fix","read":"<p>Pick the best response to each problem. Think about which pillar is at risk.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q3","type":"mc","q":"Royalty numbers keep getting changed by accident when two people edit the same spreadsheet. Which tool protects the <b>integrity</b> of that data?","opts":["Version history that shows who changed what","A second internet provider for the building","A private setting on every employee account","A longer Wi-Fi password in the office"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mc","q":"Support agents need to read customer account details quickly during a live outage. Which change would protect <b>availability</b>?","opts":["Turn off two-factor authentication for everyone","Keep working backups and a second way into the tools","Delete the oldest customer accounts to save space","Hide all customer data from every employee at once"]},{"id":"q5","type":"mc","q":"A manager wants a new rule: every change to payment data needs three approvals. What is the likely tradeoff?","opts":["Less confidentiality and less integrity for the data","No tradeoff, because extra approvals never cost anything","More protection, but slower access when it is urgent","More availability and a lot less integrity overall"]}]},{"h":"Write back to your lead","read":"<p>Maya wants a short note she can forward. Write two to three sentences that cover each part below.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q6","type":"write","q":"Pick one incident from the first task. Name the pillar that failed, say what it means in plain words, and give one thing Spotify could do to prevent it.","min":30}]}]}},"1.2":{"id":"1.2","title":"OSINT: Open Source Intelligence","back":"../unit-1/index.html","w":{"pts":50,"intro":"You will go through the OSINT guide one part at a time. Every step names the part to open, then asks questions that only someone who read it can answer. You need 100% to open the assignment, and you can check as many times as you like.","steps":[{"h":"Part 00: What is OSINT?","read":"<p>Open the guide and read <b>00: What Is OSINT?</b> from the first paragraph to the end of the section, including \"The definition\" and \"What open source means.\"</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the 1.2 OSINT guide","href":"../unit-1/1-2-osint-guide.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q1","type":"mc","q":"What does OSINT stand for?","opts":["Online Security Intelligence","Open Systems Investigation","Official Source Information","Open Source Intelligence"]},{"id":"q2","type":"mc","q":"What does \"open source\" mean in this guide?","opts":["Available to anyone who knows where to look","Hacked by a person with special tools","Leaked from a company's private servers","Free to copy without any credit at all"]},{"id":"q3","type":"mc","q":"What do hackers almost always do before they try to break into a system?","opts":["Buy a new computer and a new account","OSINT, to learn about their target first","Delete the logs from the target system","Ask the target for permission to test it"]}]},{"h":"Part 01: The five main sources","read":"<p>Read <b>01: The Five Main Sources</b>. Notice the example sites named in each box.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the 1.2 OSINT guide","href":"../unit-1/1-2-osint-guide.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q4","type":"match","q":"Match each source to the example the guide gives for it.","left":["Search engines and the open web","Public records and government databases","News, blogs and archives","Data aggregators and people-search sites"],"right":["Old pages saved on the Wayback Machine","Court records, property deeds and voter rolls","Shodan, which searches internet-connected devices","FastPeopleSearch, Spokeo and BeenVerified"]},{"id":"q5","type":"mc","q":"According to \"The combined effect,\" how long can it take a stranger to build a frighteningly complete picture of you?","opts":["A full month of work using paid tools only","About a week of careful, patient research work","Under an hour, without hacking anything","It cannot be done without a large data breach"]}]},{"h":"Part 02: Who uses OSINT?","read":"<p>Read <b>02: Who Uses OSINT?</b>. It sorts people into three groups: the good guys, everyday users, and the concerning side.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the 1.2 OSINT guide","href":"../unit-1/1-2-osint-guide.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q6","type":"match","q":"Match each user to what the guide says they do with OSINT.","left":["Journalists","Cybersecurity professionals","Scammers","Stalkers and doxxers"],"right":["Learn names and schedules to write a fake email that looks real","Verify stories and uncover things people in power hide","Find what an attacker would see first, then fix it","Find a home address, school and family"]},{"id":"q7","type":"text","q":"What is it called when a scammer uses OSINT to write a fake email that looks shockingly real to one specific person?","ph":"Two words"},{"id":"q8","type":"mc","q":"The guide says OSINT is like a kitchen knife. What changes between a journalist and a stalker?","opts":["The information, because the sources differ","The law, because stalking is always legal","The tools, because only experts own them","The intent, because the information is identical"]}]},{"h":"Part 03: Five public posts","read":"<p>Read <b>03: A Real Example</b> about Jordan's five public Instagram posts. For each post, notice what a stranger learns.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the 1.2 OSINT guide","href":"../unit-1/1-2-osint-guide.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q9","type":"mc","q":"Why does the guide say a dog's name in a photo caption matters?","opts":["Dog names are often used in passwords","The dog's name proves who owns the account","Dogs have to be registered with the police","Strangers can then see the dog's home address"]},{"id":"q10","type":"mc","q":"Why does a \"happy birthday Mom!\" post give away useful information?","opts":["It shows exactly when the whole family is on vacation","Mom's name and birthday are common security questions","Instagram sells the birthday to the school district","Birthdays are always listed in public court records"]},{"id":"q11","type":"text","q":"The hidden data inside a photo file that can include the exact GPS coordinates has a four-letter name. What is it?","ph":"Four letters"},{"id":"q12","type":"num","q":"The guide says that with zero hacking a stranger learns all of this in under how many minutes?"}]},{"h":"Part 04: OSINT and the CIA Triad","read":"<p>Read <b>04: OSINT and the CIA Triad</b>. Each pillar has a real example.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the 1.2 OSINT guide","href":"../unit-1/1-2-osint-guide.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q13","type":"match","q":"Match each pillar to the real example the guide gives for how OSINT strains it.","left":["Confidentiality","Integrity","Availability"],"right":["You delete a tweet but it was already screenshotted and archived","A typo in a public arrest record names the wrong person","A private thought you posted at 14 is still Googleable at 25"]},{"id":"q14","type":"mc","q":"In Part 04, why is availability a problem for privacy?","opts":["Only the owner can ever reach the data again later","Sites go offline so often that nobody can rely on them","Public data is too available: it is copied everywhere","Public data is never available when you need it most"]}]},{"h":"Part 05: Registries, blotters and aggregators","read":"<p>Read <b>05: Case Study: Registries and Blotters</b>, including the three \"Where Each Pillar Breaks\" boxes and the table.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the 1.2 OSINT guide","href":"../unit-1/1-2-osint-guide.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q15","type":"mc","q":"What is a police blotter, according to the guide?","opts":["A tool that quietly deletes old arrest records for you","A national list of every person ever convicted of a crime","A private file that is kept only by police detectives","A daily news feed of arrests and calls for service"]},{"id":"q16","type":"multi","q":"Which are the three ways data aggregators get your data?","opts":["Buying and selling data","Public records","Reading your private texts","Guessing your password","Social media scraping"]},{"id":"q17","type":"mc","q":"The guide says a typo on a blotter lists the wrong name for an arrest. Which pillar failed?","opts":["Integrity, because the data must be true to be fair","None, because the data was public anyway","Availability, because the blotter went offline","Confidentiality, because the arrest was secret"]},{"id":"q18","type":"mc","q":"Why might someone proven innocent still find their mugshot hard to remove?","opts":["Mugshots are protected by strict copyright law","Some sites charge a fee, and archives keep copies","Search engines do not accept any removal requests","Courts are not allowed to delete any record at all"]}]},{"h":"Part 07: Defend yourself","read":"<p>Read <b>07: Defend Yourself</b> and the closing paragraph about hygiene.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the 1.2 OSINT guide","href":"../unit-1/1-2-osint-guide.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q19","type":"mc","q":"What is the goal of OSINT hygiene, according to the guide?","opts":["Deleting every account that you have ever made","Hiding from every single person who might search you","Making sure what is out there is what you chose","Becoming completely invisible on the whole internet"]},{"id":"q20","type":"write","q":"In your own words, explain how five posts that each look harmless can still be risky. Use Jordan as your example. (2 to 3 sentences)","min":25}]}]},"a":{"pts":100,"company":"Zillow","color":"#006AFF","tagline":"You are a trust and safety intern. Your team checks what a stranger could learn from public information before a new feature launches.","from":"Dana Ortiz, Trust and Safety Manager","subject":"Review a sample profile before we launch the new seller page","email":"<p>Hi, we are adding a seller page that shows an agent's name, listings and a short bio. Before launch I want a safety review.</p><p>Use what you know about OSINT. Our test person is Riley, a 17-year-old who volunteers for a family friend who sells homes. All of Riley's posts are public. Work through the tasks in order.</p>","steps":[{"h":"What does a stranger learn?","read":"<p>Riley posted: (1) a photo in a school hoodie at a Friday football game, (2) a selfie in the driveway with the house number visible, (3) \"Mom's birthday dinner on the 14th!\", (4) \"Heading to the lake all weekend with the whole family,\" (5) a story saying \"AP test Thursday 2nd period.\"</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q1","type":"match","q":"Match each post to the main thing a stranger learns from it.","left":["The school hoodie at the football game","The driveway selfie with the house number","Mom's birthday dinner on the 14th","Heading to the lake all weekend","AP test Thursday 2nd period"],"right":["Riley's school and a day the team plays","Where Riley will be at what time","The house will be empty","Where Riley lives","A birthday and a family name"]},{"id":"q2","type":"mc","q":"Post 4 (the weekend at the lake) is the riskiest of the five. Why?","opts":["It reveals Riley's full legal name to strangers","It shows Riley's face to the entire public","It names a lake somewhere in the state of Georgia","It tells everyone that nobody is home for a while"]}]},{"h":"Where the information comes from","read":"<p>Sort the sources a researcher would use to build a profile of an agent or a seller.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q3","type":"match","q":"Match each source type to something Zillow's reviewers might find there.","left":["Public records","Social media","Data aggregators","News and archives"],"right":["A property deed with the owner's name","An old school newsletter on the Wayback Machine","Photos with a street sign in the background","One profile that combines address, relatives and phone"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mc","q":"A reviewer finds that a seller's home address, age and relatives all appear on a people-search site. Which sentence is the most accurate?","opts":["None of it was hacked; it was gathered from public sources","The site must have broken into private databases","It is illegal for anyone to gather this data","This is only possible for famous people"]}]},{"h":"Pillars and recommendations","read":"<p>Dana wants the team to name the pillar involved and suggest a fix.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q5","type":"mc","q":"A seller's page shows the wrong phone number because a scraper copied an old listing. Which pillar is the problem?","opts":["Confidentiality, because the phone number was private","Integrity, because the information is not accurate","None, because a phone number is already public","Availability, because the page loaded far too slowly"]},{"id":"q6","type":"multi","q":"Which changes would reduce what a stranger can learn from the seller page? Pick all that apply.","opts":["Show a business phone number instead of a home number","Hide the exact street address until a buyer is verified","Add the names of the seller's children","Show the seller's daily schedule","Strip hidden photo location data before upload"]}]},{"h":"Write the recommendation","read":"<p>Write a short note back to Dana (three or four sentences).</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q7","type":"write","q":"Using Riley as the example, explain what OSINT is and give two specific changes Riley (or the seller page) should make before launch.","min":40}]}]}},"1.3":{"id":"1.3","title":"Police and the 4th Amendment","back":"../unit-1/index.html","w":{"pts":50,"intro":"You will go through the 1.3 notes one part at a time. Each step names the part to open, then asks questions that only someone who read it can answer. You need 100% to open the assignment, and you can check as many times as you like.","steps":[{"h":"Part 00: Deception is legal","read":"<p>Read the top of the page and <b>00: Core Reality: Deception Is Legal</b>.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the 1.3 notes","href":"../unit-1/1-3-notes.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q1","type":"mc","q":"What does the notes say the 4th Amendment protects you from?","opts":["Being recorded by the police at all","Every kind of arrest by an officer","Unreasonable searches and seizures","Any question a police officer asks"]},{"id":"q2","type":"mc","q":"In Georgia and most of the U.S., what can police legally do during questioning?","opts":["Only question you when a lawyer is in the room","Only talk to you for ten minutes at a time at most","Only ask questions that are completely and fully true","Use trickery, bluffing and even lies to get a confession"]},{"id":"q3","type":"mc","q":"What are interrogations designed to do, according to the notes?","opts":["Secure a confession, not hold a casual, honest chat","Help you decide whether you need an attorney","Prove to the officer that you are innocent","Explain your rights to you step by step"]}]},{"h":"Part 01: The \"voluntary\" standard","read":"<p>Read <b>01: The \"Voluntary\" Standard</b>, both lists and \"The pattern\" box.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the 1.3 notes","href":"../unit-1/1-3-notes.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q4","type":"text","q":"Which Georgia law draws the line between allowed tactics and an involuntary confession? Type the section number, like 12-3-456.","ph":"Section number"},{"id":"q5","type":"multi","q":"Which of these tactics can police legally use, according to the notes?","opts":["Posing as a peer, buyer or seller without saying they are police","Claiming your DNA or fingerprints were found at the scene when they were not","Offering a lighter sentence if you talk","Threatening physical harm to get an answer","Claiming a friend already blamed you when nobody said anything"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mc","q":"What is an illegal \"hope of benefit\"?","opts":["Letting the suspect call a parent to ask for help","Offering a lighter sentence in exchange for talking","Telling the suspect the evidence is very strong now","Hoping that the suspect will tell the whole truth"]},{"id":"q7","type":"mc","q":"Which sentence matches \"The pattern\" in the notes?","opts":["Police may lie about anything in the room except evidence","Lying about consequences is allowed if the lie is brief","Lying about facts is allowed; lying about consequences is not","Lying about facts is never allowed in any interview room"]},{"id":"q8","type":"mc","q":"Which action ends the line for police even though you have not confessed?","opts":["Telling you that the case against you is quite strong","Showing you a photograph of a person who is a suspect","Asking you where you were on the night of last Friday","Questioning you after you clearly invoke your rights"]}]},{"h":"Part 02: Five common arenas","read":"<p>Read <b>02: Five Common Interrogation Arenas</b>. Each box has its own rule.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the 1.3 notes","href":"../unit-1/1-3-notes.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q9","type":"match","q":"Match each arena to the rule the notes gives for it.","left":["On school grounds","Traffic stop","At your door (knock and talk)","Undercover or online","Station interrogation room"],"right":["The highest-stakes arena, with the full use of pressure tactics","You never have to let them in without a warrant","Administrators have lower search rules than police","Police do not have to say they are police, even if asked","You owe only ID, registration and insurance"]},{"id":"q10","type":"mc","q":"At a knock and talk, what does the notes say about things officers see once they are inside?","opts":["What they see in plain view can be used","They must close their eyes until a warrant arrives","It can only be used after you say yes","Nothing they see can ever be used"]}]},{"h":"Part 03: How to invoke your rights","read":"<p>Read <b>03: How to Invoke Your Rights</b>, the four steps and the paragraph under them.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the 1.3 notes","href":"../unit-1/1-3-notes.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q11","type":"order","q":"Put the four steps in the order the notes shows them.","items":["Say clearly that you invoke your right to remain silent and want a lawyer","Stop talking: do not explain, guess or make small talk","All legal questioning must stop","Police questioning begins"]},{"id":"q12","type":"text","q":"Type the sentence the notes tells you to say. You can leave out punctuation.","ph":"The exact sentence"},{"id":"q13","type":"mc","q":"What is \"the part almost everyone gets wrong\"?","opts":["You must say it in a loud voice to count","Talking again can un-invoke your rights","You must wait until the officer finishes","You can only invoke your rights once in your life"]},{"id":"q14","type":"write","q":"In your own words, explain why the notes says the safest thing to say in an interrogation is also the shortest. (2 to 3 sentences)","min":25}]}]},"a":{"pts":100,"company":"Discord","color":"#5865F2","tagline":"You are a safety-education intern. Your team is writing a short \"know your rights\" guide for teen users about police and online chats.","from":"Sam Reyes, Safety Education Lead","subject":"Draft the teen \"know your rights\" card (due Friday)","email":"<p>Hi, we are building a help-center card that explains what police can and cannot do when they talk to a teenager, online or in person. It is for Georgia, so use the Georgia rules from class.</p><p>First I need you to check our draft facts, then write the part teens will actually remember. This is a classroom simulation, so the details are made up.</p>","steps":[{"h":"Check the draft facts","read":"<p>The draft card lists six police tactics. Decide which are legal.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q1","type":"multi","q":"Which of these tactics may police legally use in Georgia? Pick all that apply.","opts":["Promising a lighter sentence if the teen confesses","Continuing to question after the teen asks for a lawyer","Telling a teen that a friend already named them","Threatening to hurt the teen unless they answer","Joining a chat as a \"new friend\" without saying they are police","Telling a teen their fingerprints were found at the scene when they were not"]},{"id":"q2","type":"mc","q":"A teen reads: \"If you talk, the officer can promise you will go home today.\" What is wrong with this line?","opts":["It is true only at traffic stops","Officers may only promise freedom in writing","Promising a benefit is an illegal \"hope of benefit\"","Nothing; officers may promise anything"]}]},{"h":"Name the arena","read":"<p>The card has a short section for each place a teen might be questioned.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q3","type":"match","q":"Match each situation to the arena it belongs to.","left":["An officer knocks on the front door and asks to come in","A driver is pulled over for a broken tail light","A \"fellow gamer\" in a chat turns out to be an officer","An assistant principal looks through a backpack"],"right":["Undercover or online","School grounds","Traffic stop","Knock and talk"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mc","q":"In the driver's case, which items does the driver have to provide?","opts":["Permission to search the trunk","A full written statement","Only the car keys","ID, registration and insurance"]}]},{"h":"The script","read":"<p>The card's most important box is the script a teen should say. Sam wants the steps in order and the exact words.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q5","type":"order","q":"Put the four steps of the card in the right order.","items":["Say clearly: I am invoking my right to remain silent, and I want a lawyer","Legal questioning must stop","Stop talking and do not explain","Questioning begins"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mc","q":"A teen says the script, then starts chatting with the officer to \"clear things up.\" What happens to the protection?","opts":["The teen un-invoked their rights by talking again","A lawyer arrives automatically and takes over the talk","The officer must stop asking questions forever anyway","Nothing at all, since the rights last for life once said"]}]},{"h":"Write the card","read":"<p>Write the short text for the card (three or four sentences).</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q7","type":"write","q":"Write the text of the card for teens. Say what lying is allowed, what is not, and give the exact sentence to say.","min":40}]}]}},"1.4":{"id":"1.4","title":"Your Phone's Invisible Footprint","back":"../unit-1/index.html","w":{"pts":50,"intro":"You will go through the 1.4 lesson page and the interactive case files. In the case files you open each case, step through its evidence, and play the match game. You need 100% to open the assignment, and you can check as many times as you like.","steps":[{"h":"The lesson page","read":"<p>Open the 1.4 lesson page. Read the bell ringer and the \"Notes and Examples\" box under it.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the 1.4 lesson page","href":"../unit-1/1-4-your-phones-invisible-footprint.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q1","type":"multi","q":"Which three categories does the 1.4 page use to organize what your phone gives away?","opts":["Location","Battery life","Hidden data","Habits","Screen size"]},{"id":"q2","type":"mc","q":"How many people did BTK kill between 1974 and 1991, according to the bell ringer?","opts":["Seven","Ten","Four","Fourteen"]},{"id":"q3","type":"mc","q":"What mistake does the bell ringer say led to BTK's arrest in 2005?","opts":["A confession that he made to a friend by phone","A fingerprint left on a stolen car at a scene","A floppy disk whose hidden file data pointed to him","A photo of him that ran in a local newspaper"]}]},{"h":"Case 1: BTK (Dennis Rader)","read":"<p>Open the case files and choose <b>Dennis Rader (BTK)</b>. Step through all six steps on the <b>The evidence</b> tab and read the \"What he did not think about\" tab.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the case files","href":"../unit-1/caught-by-their-data.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q4","type":"mc","q":"How did police answer BTK's question about whether a floppy disk could be traced?","opts":["With a phone call to the TV station","With a letter from a lawyer to his home","By refusing to answer at all","With a newspaper ad saying it would be OK"]},{"id":"q5","type":"text","q":"The file's hidden properties named a church. Type the name of the church.","ph":"Name of the church"},{"id":"q6","type":"text","q":"What word do we use for hidden data about a file, such as who last saved it?","ph":"One word"},{"id":"q7","type":"order","q":"Put the BTK steps in order.","items":["Investigators read the file's hidden properties","The purple floppy disk arrives","A church website shows the council president is Dennis Rader","He asks whether a floppy disk can be traced","DNA confirms the match","He is arrested"]},{"id":"q8","type":"mc","q":"Why was it legal for police to tell him \"it will be OK\" when it was not true?","opts":["Police may lie to suspects, as lesson 1.3 showed","The ad was a private message, not a statement","He had already confessed on the phone","The law only covers lies told in a courtroom"]}]},{"h":"Case 2: Bryan Kohberger","read":"<p>Choose <b>Bryan Kohberger</b>. Step through the evidence and read the \"What he did not think about\" tab.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the case files","href":"../unit-1/caught-by-their-data.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q9","type":"mc","q":"In which window of time did his phone not connect to cell towers near the home?","opts":["About noon to 2 p.m.","About 3 a.m. to 5 a.m.","About 9 a.m. to 11 a.m.","About 7 p.m. to 9 p.m."]},{"id":"q10","type":"mc","q":"What does the case file mean by \"an absence is also data\"?","opts":["A deleted app erases every one of its records for good","A missing text message can never be recovered by anyone","A phone that stops connecting leaves a gap in the log","A phone that is turned off cannot be traced by any means"]},{"id":"q11","type":"text","q":"In which month and year did the four University of Idaho students die? Type it as month and year.","ph":"Month and year"},{"id":"q12","type":"mc","q":"What did the phone data do on its own in the case?","opts":["It was ignored by investigators because it was a gap","It replaced the need for a warrant in every county","It proved the whole case against him all by itself","It fit together with the car, the DNA and earlier visits"]}]},{"h":"Match the evidence","read":"<p>Open the other six case files and read each one. Then open the <b>Match the evidence</b> tab and play the game.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the case files","href":"../unit-1/caught-by-their-data.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q13","type":"match","q":"Match each case to the data that caught or cleared the person.","left":["BTK (Rader)","Kohberger","Golden State Killer","Ross Ulbricht","John McAfee","Reality Winner","Richard Dabate","Rodney Bradford"],"right":["GPS coordinates inside a photo","Tracking dots printed on a page","A cousin's DNA on a genealogy site","A Facebook server record","An old forum post with an email address","A step counter on a wrist","Cell-tower records: a gap and a pattern","Hidden file properties on a floppy disk"]},{"id":"q14","type":"mc","q":"What two things do the printer tracking dots in the Reality Winner case encode?","opts":["The printer's serial number and the date and time","The text of the page and the name of the person who read it","The paper brand and the price of the ink that was used","The name of the user who printed it and her home address"]},{"id":"q15","type":"mc","q":"What made the Rodney Bradford case different from the others?","opts":["Nobody was ever arrested","The data cleared an innocent person","It involved a printer and a laser","The data was wrong and was thrown out"]}]},{"h":"The fair warning","read":"<p>Read the last section, \"The fair-warning part: this is about you too,\" and the five audit questions.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the case files","href":"../unit-1/caught-by-their-data.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q16","type":"write","q":"Pick one of the three categories (Location, Habits, Hidden data) and explain, in your own words, how it could be used against an ordinary person. Give one example from the case files. (2 to 3 sentences)","min":25}]}]},"a":{"pts":100,"company":"Apple","color":"#1d1d1f","tagline":"You are a privacy-settings intern. The team is writing a short guide to help teens see what their iPhone quietly records.","from":"Jordan Lee, Privacy Education Team","subject":"Audit the three kinds of phone data for the teen guide","email":"<p>Hi, we need a clear, honest guide for teens about the information a phone keeps without being asked. We use three categories: <b>Location</b>, <b>Habits</b> and <b>Hidden data</b>.</p><p>First sort a list, then connect each category to a real case from class, then give advice. This is a classroom simulation, so the details are made up.</p>","steps":[{"h":"Sort the data","read":"<p>Decide which of the three categories each item belongs to.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q1","type":"match","q":"Match each item to its category.","left":["Cell-tower connections near a home","The apps you open every night at 11 p.m.","GPS coordinates saved inside a photo file"],"right":["Location","Hidden data","Habits"]},{"id":"q2","type":"mc","q":"A teen turns off location tagging for the camera app. Which category does this shrink?","opts":["Only the habits category and never anything about photos","Only the cell-tower records that the carrier keeps","Hidden data in the photos, and the location in them","Nothing at all, since photos never hold any locations"]}]},{"h":"Connect the cases","read":"<p>The guide uses real cases as warnings. Match each case to the lesson it teaches.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q3","type":"match","q":"Match each case to the lesson it teaches teens.","left":["BTK","Kohberger","Golden State Killer","Rodney Bradford"],"right":["A missing signal is also evidence","Files carry hidden metadata you never type","A trail can protect the innocent","Relatives' choices can reveal your DNA"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mc","q":"A teen says: \"If I turn my phone off, I disappear.\" Which case shows the problem with that idea?","opts":["BTK: a floppy disk with a hidden file was mailed in","Bradford: a post on a website proved he was at home","Winner: a color printer added tiny dots to the page","Kohberger: the carrier logged the connections around it"]}]},{"h":"Advice to teens","read":"<p>Jordan wants two concrete tips and one honest warning.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q5","type":"multi","q":"Which tips belong in the guide? Pick all that fit what you learned.","opts":["Search yourself the way an investigator would","Turn off location tagging for the camera","Check what an app strips from photos before posting an original","Post the original file so nothing gets lost","Delete the cell-phone plan to avoid logs"]},{"id":"q6","type":"write","q":"Write the guide's short warning (three or four sentences). Name the three categories, give one real case, and say why this matters even for people who did nothing wrong.","min":45}]}]}},"1.5":{"id":"1.5","title":"How Strong Is Your Password?","back":"../unit-1/index.html","w":{"pts":50,"intro":"You will go through the 1.5 password notes: seven attack terms, three defenses and the password lab at the bottom of the page. The lab questions need you to actually type into it. You need 100% to open the assignment, and you can check as many times as you like.","steps":[{"h":"The attacks","read":"<p>Open the notes and read the left side, <b>How Attackers Crack Passwords</b>. Read every card.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the 1.5 notes","href":"../unit-1/1-5-notes.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q1","type":"match","q":"Match each attack term to what the notes says it is.","left":["Brute-force attack","Dictionary attack","Credential stuffing","Keylogger","Hash and rainbow table"],"right":["Trying one site's leaked username and password on many other sites","Software that secretly records every key you press","Trying every possible combination, one by one, until one works","A lookup chart of pre-computed scrambled versions of common passwords","Starting from real words and leaked lists, then adding predictable tweaks"]},{"id":"q2","type":"mc","q":"According to the notes, what actually decides how many guesses an attacker needs?","opts":["Entropy: how unpredictable the password is","How complicated the password looks to a person","How long ago the password was last changed","How many symbols the password happens to have"]},{"id":"q3","type":"mc","q":"Why does leetspeak (P@ssw0rd!) barely slow an attacker down?","opts":["Attackers cannot type symbols into login pages","Cracking tools check those swaps automatically","Hashes ignore any characters that are not letters","Sites reject passwords that contain an at-sign"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mc","q":"Why is no password \"strong enough\" to beat a keylogger?","opts":["Keyloggers can only be used on short passwords","A strong password is blocked by the keylogger","The attacker never has to guess; they read what you typed","Keyloggers only work when a site has no hashing"]},{"id":"q5","type":"text","q":"Sites do not store your actual password. They store a scrambled version called a what?","ph":"One word"}]},{"h":"The defenses","read":"<p>Read the right side, <b>What Actually Defends You</b>, and the closing line about \"7 ways in, 3 ways to actually stop them.\"</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the 1.5 notes","href":"../unit-1/1-5-notes.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q6","type":"match","q":"Match each defense to what the notes says it does.","left":["MFA","Passphrase","Password manager"],"right":["A second proof of identity beyond the password, such as a code on your phone","Makes and remembers a unique random password for every site","A string of random, unrelated words instead of one word with substitutions"]},{"id":"q7","type":"mc","q":"Which attack does a password manager solve, according to the notes?","opts":["The problem of keyloggers that record every key you press","The problem of sites that keep no passwords on file at all","The problem of rainbow tables being published on the internet","The reuse problem that makes credential stuffing possible"]},{"id":"q8","type":"mc","q":"The notes count how many ways in and how many ways to stop them. What are the counts?","opts":["7 ways in and 3 ways to stop them","10 ways in and 1 way to stop them","3 ways in and 7 ways to stop them","5 ways in and 5 ways to stop them"]},{"id":"q9","type":"mc","q":"What does the notes say beats trying to out-clever any single attack?","opts":["Hiding your username on every single site that you use","Layering a passphrase, a password manager and MFA together","Picking one very long and complex password for every site","Changing your one password every single week of the year"]}]},{"h":"The password lab","read":"<p>Scroll to <b>Try it: crack your own made-up password</b>. Use made-up passwords only, never a real one. Type each test password into the lab and read what it says.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the 1.5 notes","href":"../unit-1/1-5-notes.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q10","type":"mc","q":"Type <code>P@ssw0rd!</code> into the lab. What does it say?","opts":["It needs about 4 days of nonstop guessing to crack","It needs more than a billion years of guessing to crack","Cracked instantly: a common word with simple tricks","It cannot be tested at all because it contains a symbol"]},{"id":"q11","type":"num","q":"Type <code>Qx7#vLm2</code> into the lab. About how many bits does it report?","ph":"A whole number"},{"id":"q12","type":"num","q":"Type <code>river-comet-anchor-maple</code> (four random words) into the lab. About how many bits does it report?","ph":"A whole number"},{"id":"q13","type":"mc","q":"What does comparing those two results show?","opts":["Short passwords are always better than any long passwords","Symbols are always better than a string of random words","Words can never be as strong as a group of symbols","Four random words are about as strong as 8 random characters"]},{"id":"q14","type":"write","q":"In your own words, explain why layering a passphrase, a password manager and MFA beats trying to make one clever password. (2 to 3 sentences)","min":25}]}]},"a":{"pts":100,"company":"Netflix","color":"#E50914","tagline":"You are a security intern on the accounts team. Your lead wants a plan to stop stolen passwords from turning into stolen accounts.","from":"Priya Natarajan, Accounts Security Lead","subject":"Why are accounts getting taken over? Review three incident reports","email":"<p>Hi, we are seeing viewers lose access to their accounts even though our own servers were never hacked. I need you to label what is happening and recommend defenses.</p><p>This is a classroom simulation, so the details are made up. Work in order.</p>","steps":[{"h":"Name the attack","read":"<p>For each report, choose the attack that fits best.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q1","type":"match","q":"Match each report to the attack.","left":["A viewer reused the same password on a gaming site that was breached, and criminals logged in to the streaming account with it.","A program tries thousands of real words, each with a capital letter and the number 123 added.","Hidden software on a family computer sends everything the viewer types to a stranger.","A program tries every possible combination of four characters."],"right":["Dictionary attack","Brute-force attack","Keylogger","Credential stuffing"]}]},{"h":"Choose the best defense","read":"<p>Priya wants the single best fix for each problem.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q2","type":"mc","q":"Viewers reuse one password on many sites. Which tool fixes that most directly?","opts":["A password manager that makes a unique password per site","Changing the same password once every single year","A password with an at-sign swapped in for the letter a","A longer password that is reused on every site"]},{"id":"q3","type":"mc","q":"An attacker already has a viewer's exact password. Which defense can still stop the login?","opts":["Making the username much longer than before","MFA, a second proof such as a code on the phone","Replacing every letter o in the password with a zero","Adding a capital letter to the end of the password"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mc","q":"A viewer thinks <code>P@ssw0rd!</code> is clever. What is the best response?","opts":["It is strong, because it has a capital letter","It is weak only if the viewer writes it down","Cracking tools check those swaps automatically, so it is weak","It is strong, because it has four kinds of characters"]}]},{"h":"Use the lab","read":"<p>Open the <a href=\"../unit-1/1-5-notes.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">password lab in the 1.5 notes</a> and test the four made-up passwords below. Use only these made-up passwords.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q5","type":"mc","q":"Test <code>P@ssw0rd!</code>, <code>kittycat</code>, <code>Qx7#vLm2</code> and <code>river-comet-anchor-maple-tiger</code>. Which one does the lab say takes the longest to crack?","opts":["kittycat","Qx7#vLm2","P@ssw0rd!","river-comet-anchor-maple-tiger"]},{"id":"q6","type":"num","q":"How many bits does the lab report for <code>river-comet-anchor-maple-tiger</code>?","ph":"A whole number"}]},{"h":"Write the recommendation","read":"<p>Write the plan Priya will send to viewers (three or four sentences).</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q7","type":"write","q":"Write the advice for viewers. Name at least two defenses, say which attack each one stops, and say why a password with symbol swaps is not enough.","min":45}]}]}},"1.6":{"id":"1.6","title":"How Hackers Track You Online","back":"../unit-1/index.html","w":{"pts":50,"intro":"You will go through the 1.6 notes, \"Who's Watching You,\" one section at a time. One step needs you to run the ad auction demo on the page. You need 100% to open the assignment, and you can check as many times as you like.","steps":[{"h":"The three layers","read":"<p>Open the notes and read the top of the page down to <b>1: The hardware</b>. The page splits tracking into three layers.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the 1.6 notes","href":"../unit-1/whos-watching-you.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q1","type":"match","q":"Match each layer to what the notes says it is.","left":["Layer 1: Hardware","Layer 2: Software","Layer 3: The market"],"right":["The apps you install and the permissions you give them","Brokers who buy and resell data about you","Built-in sensors that are always on"]},{"id":"q2","type":"mc","q":"Which layer does the page say most people only think about?","opts":["The middle one, the apps and their permissions","The first one, the phone's sensors","The last one, the data market","None of them, since people never think about it"]}]},{"h":"1: The hardware","read":"<p>Read section <b>1: The hardware</b>. Every sensor card has \"Why it's there\" and \"What it can do to you.\"</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the 1.6 notes","href":"../unit-1/whos-watching-you.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q3","type":"match","q":"Match each sensor to what the page says it can do to you.","left":["LiDAR scanner","Barometer","TrueDepth camera","Ambient light sensor","Accelerometer"],"right":["Reads micro-expressions to see whether an ad made you happy","Scans the 3D layout and furniture of your bedroom","Estimates your family's income from the floor you live on","Tells an app whether you are in bed or outside","Identifies you by the rhythm of your walk"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mc","q":"What reason does the page give for the barometer being in the phone?","opts":["It measures how fast your phone is moving through the air","It tells 911 what floor you are on inside a tall building","It checks that your charger is a genuine one","It helps your phone find free Wi-Fi inside a building"]},{"id":"q5","type":"mc","q":"According to the notes, what does the accelerometer data let a researcher do?","opts":["Read the private messages that the person sends each day","Tell exactly which apps the person has installed on it","Identify a specific person from their walking pattern","Find the password that the person types in at login"]}]},{"h":"2: The apps","read":"<p>Read section <b>2: The apps</b>. Notice what the page says the policy says, and what it admits the policy does not say.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the 1.6 notes","href":"../unit-1/whos-watching-you.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q6","type":"mc","q":"What does the notes say TikTok's U.S. privacy policy says it may collect from the videos you make?","opts":["Your exact password for every one of your other apps","Your bank account number and your card number","Your contacts' private text messages and photos","Biometric identifiers such as faceprints and voiceprints"]},{"id":"q7","type":"mc","q":"What does the page tell you to do before you hand an app your face and voice?","opts":["Read the app's privacy policy","Turn on airplane mode first","Delete your account first","Ask a friend to try it first"]},{"id":"q8","type":"text","q":"What does the page say you should check on Instagram's map feature, because it is easy to leave on? Type one word.","ph":"One word"}]},{"h":"3: The market","read":"<p>Read section <b>3: The market</b>. Then scroll down to <b>Try it: watch an ad auction</b>, press the button and read the result.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the 1.6 notes","href":"../unit-1/whos-watching-you.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q9","type":"num","q":"The page says a 2022 study estimated a typical American's data is exposed in ad auctions about how many times a day?"},{"id":"q10","type":"num","q":"In the ad auction demo, how many companies receive your location and ad ID?"},{"id":"q11","type":"text","q":"In the demo, which company wins the ad slot?","ph":"Company name"},{"id":"q12","type":"mc","q":"In the pharmacy example, what happens to the bidders who lose the auction?","opts":["They have to delete the data at once","They may keep the data they received","They are fined by the government","They are told whether they lost"]},{"id":"q13","type":"order","q":"Put the warrant loophole in order.","items":["The government buys the data from the broker","No warrant was ever required","Your app sells your location data to a broker"]}]},{"h":"4 and 5: Lock it down and see it yourself","read":"<p>Read section <b>4: Lock it down</b>, then <b>5: See it yourself</b>, including the Incognito myth.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the 1.6 notes","href":"../unit-1/whos-watching-you.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q14","type":"match","q":"Match each setting to what it switches off or limits.","left":["Precise Location off for social apps","Motion and Fitness off for social apps","Allow Apps to Request to Track off","Audit every permission"],"right":["Kills the advertising ID that feeds the auction","Shuts down identifying you by your walk","Keeps apps from learning your exact room","Removes access an app does not need to work"]},{"id":"q15","type":"mc","q":"According to the notes, what was Incognito mode built to hide?","opts":["Your identity from every one of the ad networks","Your exact location from every website you visit","Your history from other people using your computer","Your browser fingerprint from the trackers on a site"]},{"id":"q16","type":"mc","q":"Why does the page warn you to use the exact CreepJS address and no other?","opts":["Other addresses are blocked by every school","The real address is the only one that loads fast","The developer charges a fee at other addresses","Copycat sites run modified code to capture your fingerprint"]},{"id":"q17","type":"write","q":"In your own words, explain the idea behind \"If the app is free, the price is the map of your life.\" Use one example from the page. (2 to 3 sentences)","min":25}]}]},"a":{"pts":100,"company":"Instagram","color":"#C13584","tagline":"You are a product-safety intern. The team is reviewing a map feature that shows teens' friends where they are.","from":"Alex Rivera, Product Safety Manager","subject":"Review the new location sharing screen before it ships","email":"<p>Hi, our new map feature lets a teen share where they are with friends. Before launch I want you to look at the data it touches and how the settings should work.</p><p>Use what you learned about the three layers of tracking. This is a classroom simulation, so the details are made up.</p>","steps":[{"h":"Which layer is it?","read":"<p>Sort each piece of the feature into the layer it belongs to.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q1","type":"match","q":"Match each part of the feature to its layer.","left":["The phone's built-in sensors that detect where you are","The permission screen that asks to turn on location","A company that buys location data and resells it"],"right":["The market","Software","Hardware"]},{"id":"q2","type":"mc","q":"A teen's phone says \"Precise Location: On\" for the map. What does precise location give the app?","opts":["Your exact spot, not just your general area","Only the name of your cell carrier","Only the country that you live in","Nothing, because maps do not use location"]}]},{"h":"What can go wrong?","read":"<p>Alex wants three risks explained in plain words.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q3","type":"mc","q":"A teen opens the app in a doctor's waiting room, and the location goes into an ad auction. What may happen to the bidders who lose?","opts":["Only the winner of the auction can ever see the data","They may keep the data, so more companies know where they were","The data is deleted the moment the auction ends","Nobody sees the data until a court orders that it be shared"]},{"id":"q4","type":"order","q":"A government agency wants a teen's location history. Put the steps of the warrant loophole in order.","items":["The agency buys the data from the broker","An app sells location data to a broker","No warrant is ever required"]},{"id":"q5","type":"mc","q":"Why is a phone's walking rhythm a privacy risk?","opts":["It reveals the passwords that you use for your accounts","It shows the exact words that you speak out loud","It can identify a specific person, like a fingerprint","It always matches every person in exactly the same way"]}]},{"h":"Set the defaults","read":"<p>Pick the safe default settings for the launch.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q6","type":"multi","q":"Which settings should be off by default for teens? Pick all that apply.","opts":["Precise location sharing with friends","Tracking across other apps","The ability to report a problem","Motion and Fitness access","The account password"]},{"id":"q7","type":"write","q":"Write the launch recommendation (three or four sentences). Name two risks from the tracking layers, give one safe default setting, and say why \"the app is free\" matters.","min":45}]}]}},"1.7":{"id":"1.7","title":"Copyright and Ethics","back":"../unit-1/index.html","w":{"pts":50,"intro":"You will go through the Copyright Case File one case at a time. Every step names the case to open, then asks questions that only someone who read it can answer. You need 100% to open the assignment, and you can check as many times as you like.","steps":[{"h":"Case 01: What copyright covers","read":"<p>Open the case file and read the top of the page and <b>Case 01: What copyright actually covers</b>.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the 1.7 case file","href":"../unit-1/copyright-case-file.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q1","type":"mc","q":"When does your work become protected by copyright?","opts":["After you register it with a government office","After one year of public use of the work","After you put the copyright symbol on it","The moment you make it, with no paperwork or fee"]},{"id":"q2","type":"multi","q":"Which of these does the notes say are NOT protected by copyright?","opts":["An idea alone, like a story idea about a superhero","Code you wrote for a game","Facts such as the sky is blue","A photo you took","Titles, names or short phrases"]},{"id":"q3","type":"mc","q":"What exactly does copyright protect?","opts":["The exact words, notes or pixels, not the idea behind them","The idea behind your work, in any form that it takes","Only work that has already been sold to the public","Any other work that is about the same general subject"]},{"id":"q4","type":"match","q":"Match each exclusive right to what it means.","left":["Copy it","Share it","Display it","Perform it","Remix it"],"right":["Change it into something new (a derivative work)","Play or perform it out loud","Distribute or post it","Make more copies","Show it publicly"]}]},{"h":"Case 02: The fair use test","read":"<p>Read <b>Case 02: The fair use test</b>, the four factors and the table of real posts.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the 1.7 case file","href":"../unit-1/copyright-case-file.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q5","type":"match","q":"Match each fair use factor to the question it asks.","left":["Factor 1","Factor 2","Factor 3","Factor 4"],"right":["Does your use hurt sales of the original?","How much of the work did you use?","Why are you using it? Is it transformative?","What kind of work is it, factual or creative?"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mc","q":"Which factor does the notes say courts and platforms weigh the heaviest?","opts":["Factor 3: how much you used","Factor 4: does it hurt sales","Factor 1: why you are using it","Factor 2: what kind of work it is"]},{"id":"q7","type":"mc","q":"How does the notes rate a movie reaction video that plays almost an entire 20-minute video?","opts":["Public domain","Usually fair use","Usually not fair use","Always fair use"]},{"id":"q8","type":"mc","q":"Which is usually fair use, according to the table?","opts":["Reposting an artist's picture online without giving any credit","A full song used as background music, completely unedited","Posting someone else's whole video and calling it your own","A review with a few short clips and your own analysis"]}]},{"h":"Case 03: Public domain and Creative Commons","read":"<p>Read <b>Case 03: Public domain and Creative Commons</b>, including the four license cards.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the 1.7 case file","href":"../unit-1/copyright-case-file.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q9","type":"mc","q":"Why can you use any NASA image for free?","opts":["U.S. federal government work is public domain","NASA images all use the CC BY-NC license","NASA owns every image but promises it never sues anyone","All images taken in outer space are public domain"]},{"id":"q10","type":"match","q":"Match each Creative Commons license to what it requires.","left":["CC BY","CC BY-SA","CC BY-NC","CC0"],"right":["Credit the creator by name","Credit the creator and do not use it to make money","Credit the creator and share your remix under the same license","The creator gives up every right; use it however you want"]},{"id":"q11","type":"mc","q":"What does \"royalty-free\" usually mean, according to the notes?","opts":["The work is free to use for anything","You pay once instead of paying every time","The work is in the public domain","The creator cannot ever be paid"]},{"id":"q12","type":"mc","q":"Which of these is a legit free-to-use source named in the notes?","opts":["Google Images","A random fan page","Wikimedia Commons","A friend's camera roll"]}]},{"h":"Cases 04 to 06: Mistakes, plagiarism and consequences","read":"<p>Read <b>Case 04: Where teens actually break the rules</b>, <b>Case 05: Plagiarism vs. copyright</b> and <b>Case 06: What happens if you get it wrong</b>.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the 1.7 case file","href":"../unit-1/copyright-case-file.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q13","type":"mc","q":"A teen posts a video with a trending song from TikTok. They then download the video and repost it elsewhere. Why is the music no longer covered?","opts":["Music licenses on every app expire after exactly one day","Songs are in the public domain on all other sites","The teen must pay the platform a fee to reuse the song","The platform's license does not travel with the video"]},{"id":"q14","type":"mc","q":"How does the notes rate fan art and fan fiction?","opts":["Technically infringement, but usually tolerated until it is sold","Always fully legal, because it is covered by fair use","Never tolerated by any creator or any studio at all","Public domain automatically after exactly one full year"]},{"id":"q15","type":"mc","q":"What does the U.S. Copyright Office say about a fully AI-generated image?","opts":["It belongs to the person who typed the prompt","It cannot be copyrighted by anyone","It belongs to the company that made the tool","It is protected for 95 years"]},{"id":"q16","type":"match","q":"Match each problem to what solves it.","left":["Plagiarism","Copyright infringement","Takedown on a platform"],"right":["Permission, a license, or a fair use argument","Citing your source","The copyright holder files a DMCA claim"]},{"id":"q17","type":"write","q":"In your own words, explain why citing a source stops plagiarism but does not stop copyright infringement. (2 to 3 sentences)","min":25}]}]},"a":{"pts":100,"company":"YouTube","color":"#FF0000","tagline":"You are a creator-support intern. Your team helps young creators avoid takedowns, mutes and strikes.","from":"Marcus Bell, Creator Support Lead","subject":"Four creator questions for this week's help article","email":"<p>Hi, I am writing a short help article for teen creators. Each question below came from a real kind of post. I need you to give the right answer so the article is accurate.</p><p>This is a classroom simulation, so the posts are made up.</p>","steps":[{"h":"Fair use check","read":"<p>Use the four factors: why you used it, what kind of work it is, how much you used, and whether it hurts sales.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q1","type":"match","q":"Match each post to the likely verdict from the notes.","left":["A short parody over a few clips with your own jokes","Almost an entire 20-minute video with a few comments","A cropped movie screenshot with a joke caption"],"right":["Usually not fair use","A grey area, usually tolerated","Usually fair use"]},{"id":"q2","type":"mc","q":"A creator uses a full song, unedited, as background music in a public video. Which factor hurts them most?","opts":["Factor 1, because music is never transformative","None, because the song is popular","Factor 3, because they used the whole thing","Factor 2, because songs are always facts"]}]},{"h":"Which license?","read":"<p>Marcus has four photos that creators want to reuse.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q3","type":"match","q":"Match each situation to the right license or status.","left":["A photo you may use if you credit the photographer, even for a paid ad","A photo free for a school video but not for a paid ad","A photo made by a NASA employee as part of their job","A photo the creator waived every right on, on purpose"],"right":["Public domain (U.S. government work)","CC0","CC BY","CC BY-NC"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mc","q":"A stock site says \"royalty-free.\" What should a creator do?","opts":["Assume that it is in the public domain already","Credit the site and then ignore the license terms","Use it anywhere at all with no limits on it","Read the actual license, because royalty-free is not free"]}]},{"h":"Plagiarism or copyright?","read":"<p>One help-article section explains the difference. Marcus wants the cases sorted.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q5","type":"match","q":"Match each case to the problem it creates.","left":["A student copies three paragraphs of a Wikipedia article into an essay without a citation","A creator reposts another artist's work with a credit but no permission","A creator reposts another artist's work and says it is theirs"],"right":["Copyright infringement","Both problems at once","Plagiarism at school"]},{"id":"q6","type":"multi","q":"Which are real consequences a platform can apply to a creator who gets copyright wrong?","opts":["A DMCA takedown of the post","The platform deletes the creator's family photos","The platform sends a bill to the creator's school","Account strikes and a possible ban","Demonetization or a muted audio track"]}]},{"h":"Write the answer","read":"<p>Marcus needs a short answer to publish (three or four sentences).</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q7","type":"write","q":"A teen asks: \"Can I use a clip of my favorite show in my video?\" Answer using at least two fair use factors, and mention one safer option.","min":45}]}]}},"1.8":{"id":"1.8","title":"Hacking Ethics","back":"../unit-1/index.html","w":{"pts":50,"intro":"You will go through the Rules of Engagement briefing one part at a time. One step needs you to play the color game on the page. You need 100% to open the assignment, and you can check as many times as you like.","steps":[{"h":"The big idea","read":"<p>Open the briefing and read the top of the page, down to \"Every Crime Has a Category.\"</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the 1.8 briefing","href":"../unit-1/hacking-ethics-briefing.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q1","type":"mc","q":"According to the briefing, what decides whether the same computer skill is a paid job or a federal crime?","opts":["Whether someone with real authority said yes first","Whether anything was actually damaged or taken","Whether the person works for a big company","How advanced the technical skill is"]}]},{"h":"Part 00: Every crime has a category","read":"<p>Read <b>00: Every Crime Has a Category</b>. It uses killing and stealing as examples, then connects them to hacking.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the 1.8 briefing","href":"../unit-1/hacking-ethics-briefing.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q2","type":"match","q":"Match each degree of killing to its description.","left":["First-degree murder","Second-degree murder","Voluntary manslaughter","Involuntary manslaughter"],"right":["Intentional but formed in the moment, not planned","Intentional in the heat of the moment after real provocation","No intent to kill, caused by reckless or careless behavior","Intentional and planned out in advance"]},{"id":"q3","type":"match","q":"Match each type of theft to its description.","left":["Petty theft","Grand theft","Robbery","Burglary"],"right":["Taking property from a person using force or the threat of force","Taking property above that same cutoff","Entering a building with intent to commit a crime, even if nothing is taken","Taking property below a state's dollar cutoff"]},{"id":"q4","type":"match","q":"Match each color to the access it describes.","left":["Green","Amber","Red"],"right":["Grey area: nobody meant harm but nobody officially said yes","Unauthorized: nobody said yes, whatever the reason","Authorized: somebody said yes in writing before anything started"]},{"id":"q5","type":"mc","q":"The briefing says unauthorized computer access works \"exactly the same way\" as what?","opts":["Contracts, where the signature is always optional","Homicide and theft, where category changes the penalty","Copyright, where giving credit changes the law itself","Traffic tickets, where every single case is the same"]}]},{"h":"Part 01: White, grey and black hats","read":"<p>Read <b>01: White hat, grey hat, black hat</b>.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the 1.8 briefing","href":"../unit-1/hacking-ethics-briefing.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q6","type":"match","q":"Match each hat to its description.","left":["White hat","Grey hat","Black hat"],"right":["Breaks in without asking but says they meant well","Breaks in to steal, damage, spy or make illegal money","Hired or invited to find weak spots, and reports them"]},{"id":"q7","type":"mc","q":"What single question does the color of the hat answer?","opts":["Did they get paid?","Did they use a famous tool?","Did they have permission?","Did they succeed?"]},{"id":"q8","type":"mc","q":"Why can a grey hat with good intentions and a black hat with bad ones be charged under the same law?","opts":["Judges treat every hacker in exactly the same way","Grey hats always cause exactly the same damage","Good intentions are only ever a defense in a school","The law cares about permission, not how good your reason was"]},{"id":"q9","type":"mc","q":"In the briefing's example, a grey hat finds a way into a school website and then does what?","opts":["Emails the school to say they found a way in afterward","Asks the school for permission before getting in","Deletes the school's grade records without telling anyone","Sells the way in to a stranger on the internet"]}]},{"h":"Try it: what color is the access?","read":"<p>Scroll to <b>Try it: what color is the access?</b> and classify all eight situations. Then answer based on what you did.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the 1.8 briefing","href":"../unit-1/hacking-ethics-briefing.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q10","type":"num","q":"How many situations are in the color game?"},{"id":"q11","type":"text","q":"In the briefing's example of the Red category, what do you do to a friend's account \"just to look around\"? Type the one-word action.","ph":"One or two words"},{"id":"q12","type":"write","q":"In your own words, explain why a person who \"only looked\" and \"meant well\" can still be breaking the law. (2 to 3 sentences)","min":25}]}]},"a":{"pts":100,"company":"Google","color":"#4285F4","tagline":"You are an intern on a security-research outreach team. Your lead handles messages from people who say they found a problem.","from":"Lena Okafor, Security Outreach Lead","subject":"Sort this week's four security reports by permission","email":"<p>Hi, we got four messages from people who say they found weaknesses in our products. Before anyone replies, I need you to sort each one by permission, because that is what decides how we handle it.</p><p>This is a classroom simulation, so the reports are made up.</p>","steps":[{"h":"Color each report","read":"<p>Use the three colors: green is authorized, amber is a grey area, red is unauthorized.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q1","type":"match","q":"Match each report to its color.","left":["A contractor with a signed agreement tests a new app for two weeks and files a full report","A user stumbles onto a real problem on a website by accident and is not sure whether to mention it","A student guesses a classmate's password \"just to look around\" and changes nothing"],"right":["Amber: a grey area","Green: authorized","Red: unauthorized"]}]},{"h":"Name the hat","read":"<p>Lena also wants the hat for each person.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q2","type":"match","q":"Match each person to the hat.","left":["Paid to try to break into a company's website, then hands over a report","Enters a school site without asking, then emails to say it can be done","Breaks into a store's system to steal credit card numbers"],"right":["Grey hat","White hat","Black hat"]},{"id":"q3","type":"mc","q":"A researcher says: \"I never changed anything, so I did nothing wrong.\" What is the best reply?","opts":["That is a defense only if they emailed the school afterward","The law cares about permission, not how good your reason was","That is fine, because no change at all means no crime","That only matters if the website was a very small one"]}]},{"h":"Same skill, different category","read":"<p>The reply template will use the idea that categories change consequences.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q4","type":"mc","q":"Which pair is the best match for how the law treats categories?","opts":["Petty theft and grand theft are always exactly the same crime","Every killing carries exactly the same prison sentence","Two kinds of killing can carry very different penalties","Burglary only counts when something is actually taken"]},{"id":"q5","type":"write","q":"Write a short reply (three or four sentences) to a person who found a real weakness and tested it without asking. Explain what category they are in, why good intentions do not change that, and what safer step they could take next time.","min":45}]}]}},"1.9":{"id":"1.9","title":"Common Security Problems","back":"../unit-1/index.html","w":{"pts":50,"intro":"You will go through \"The Front Door\" one lock at a time. One step needs you to use the \"change one number\" demo on the page. You need 100% to open the assignment, and you can check as many times as you like.","steps":[{"h":"The four locks","read":"<p>Open the notes and read the top of the page, down to <b>01: HTTPS</b>. Four cards name the four locks.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the 1.9 notes","href":"../unit-1/the-front-door.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q1","type":"match","q":"Match each lock to the building picture the notes uses.","left":["HTTPS","CAPTCHA","2FA","OWASP"],"right":["The building code","The doorman who checks you are a person","The lock on the connection itself","The second deadbolt"]}]},{"h":"01: HTTPS","read":"<p>Read <b>01: HTTPS</b>, including the two address-bar examples and the warning about the padlock.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the 1.9 notes","href":"../unit-1/the-front-door.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q2","type":"mc","q":"According to the notes, what does plain HTTP (no S) send as readable text?","opts":["Only the pictures that appear on the page","Only your username and nothing else at all","Nothing at all, because HTTP is always private","Everything you type, like passwords and messages"]},{"id":"q3","type":"match","q":"Match each thing the padlock checks to what it does.","left":["Encryption","Certificate identity"],"right":["Confirms you are talking to the real website in the address bar","Scrambles data so nobody spying in transit can read it"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mc","q":"What does the notes say a valid padlock does NOT prove?","opts":["That the website itself is honest","That the address is spelled correctly","That your connection is private","That the page loaded at all"]}]},{"h":"02: CAPTCHA","read":"<p>Read <b>02: CAPTCHA</b>, including the five kinds.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the 1.9 notes","href":"../unit-1/the-front-door.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q5","type":"mc","q":"What is a CAPTCHA's entire job, according to the notes?","opts":["To store your login so it is ready for the next visit","To slow down programs pretending to be a real person","To scramble your password while it is in transit","To tell websites exactly who your friends are"]},{"id":"q6","type":"match","q":"Match each kind of CAPTCHA to what it asks.","left":["Distorted text","Image grid","Checkbox","Invisible","Slider or puzzle"],"right":["No test unless your behavior looks suspicious","Click every square with a traffic light or crosswalk","Type the wavy, stretched-out letters and numbers","Drag a piece into the missing spot with a smooth motion","Click I am not a robot while your mouse movement is watched"]},{"id":"q7","type":"multi","q":"Which of these does the notes say bots can do that a human cannot? Pick all that apply.","opts":["Try millions of stolen username and password pairs in minutes","Buy out a whole concert or sneaker drop in the first second","Write a school essay from memory","Hold a long face-to-face conversation","Flood comment sections with fake posts faster than any moderator can read"]}]},{"h":"03: 2FA","read":"<p>Read <b>03: 2FA</b>, the three factors and the five examples ranked from weakest to strongest.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the 1.9 notes","href":"../unit-1/the-front-door.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q8","type":"match","q":"Match each factor to its example.","left":["Something you know","Something you have","Something you are"],"right":["A password or PIN","A fingerprint or your face","A phone or a security key"]},{"id":"q9","type":"order","q":"Put these 2FA examples in order from weakest to strongest, as the notes ranks them.","items":["Security key or fingerprint","Email verification code","SMS text code","Push notification approval","Authenticator app"]},{"id":"q10","type":"text","q":"What name does the notes give to the attack where someone tricks your carrier into moving your phone number onto their phone?","ph":"Two words"}]},{"h":"04: OWASP and the demo","read":"<p>Read <b>04: OWASP</b>. Then scroll to <b>Try it</b>, use the demo that changes the number at the end of the web address, and try it with the check on and off.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the 1.9 notes","href":"../unit-1/the-front-door.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q11","type":"mc","q":"What does OWASP stand for?","opts":["Official Website Authorization System","Open World Access Security Plan","Open Web Application Security Project","Online Web Attack Safety Program"]},{"id":"q12","type":"match","q":"Match each OWASP Top 10 item to its description.","left":["#1 Broken access control","#3 Injection"],"right":["A site never checks what you typed and obeys a trick phrase","A user can see or change things they were never allowed to touch"]},{"id":"q13","type":"text","q":"In the demo, you are student 1042. Change the number to 1043. Whose grades appear?","ph":"A first name"},{"id":"q14","type":"text","q":"In the demo, turn the check on and open /1043. What error code appears?","ph":"A three-digit code"},{"id":"q15","type":"write","q":"In your own words, explain why none of the four locks can cover for the other three. Use one example from the notes. (2 to 3 sentences)","min":25}]}]},"a":{"pts":100,"company":"Ticketmaster","color":"#026CDF","tagline":"You are a web-security intern. Your team is inspecting a ticket site before a big on-sale.","from":"Chris Vance, Web Security Lead","subject":"Inspect four test pages before the on-sale starts","email":"<p>Hi, tickets for a very popular show go on sale Friday. Before then I want you to inspect the site and tell me which of the four locks is missing, working, or being attacked.</p><p>The four locks are HTTPS, CAPTCHA, 2FA and OWASP. This is a classroom simulation, so the pages and findings are made up.</p>","steps":[{"h":"Inspect the pages","read":"<p>For each test page, pick the lock that is missing, working or under attack.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q1","type":"match","q":"Match each finding to the lock it is about.","left":["The checkout page uses http:// and asks for a credit card number","A script tries ten thousand password guesses in a second with no slowdown","A leaked password cannot log in because the account also asks for a phone code","A search box runs whatever commands visitors type into it"],"right":["HTTPS","2FA","OWASP (injection)","CAPTCHA"]},{"id":"q2","type":"mc","q":"A test page shows a padlock and https://, but the address is a near-perfect misspelling of a real ticket seller. What is the right finding?","opts":["The site is safe because of the padlock","CAPTCHA is missing on the page","The padlock is fake, so https:// is broken","HTTPS is present, but the site can still be fake"]}]},{"h":"On-sale day problems","read":"<p>Chris wants to know which lock handles each on-sale problem.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q3","type":"mc","q":"Bots buy out the first 5,000 tickets in two seconds. Which lock is meant to slow them down?","opts":["CAPTCHA, the doorman","HTTPS, the lock on the connection","2FA, the second deadbolt","OWASP, the building code"]},{"id":"q4","type":"order","q":"Rank these 2FA options for the team's fans from weakest to strongest.","items":["Push approval","Text message code","Security key","Authenticator app","Email code"]},{"id":"q5","type":"mc","q":"A fan changes the number at the end of an order address and sees a stranger's tickets. Which OWASP flaw is that?","opts":["Injection","Broken access control","A CAPTCHA failure","A missing certificate"]}]},{"h":"Write the summary","read":"<p>Chris needs a short summary for the launch meeting (three or four sentences).</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q6","type":"write","q":"Write the summary. Name the four locks, say what each one protects, and give one reason a fake site can still have a padlock.","min":45}]}]}}}};
